/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A hydrodynamic journal bearing is a plain sleeve around a shaft.
   - The shaft centre sits off the bearing centre by the eccentricity e.
   - e is the journal centre shift from the bearing centre, c is the radial clearance, ε is the eccentricity ratio, and h₀ is the minimum film thickness; e, c and h₀ use the same length unit.
   - When the shaft turns, oil sticking to the journal is dragged into the clearance.
   - Three lubrication regimes matter.
   - The pressure distribution is not uniform.
   - Starts and stops cause the most wear.
   - A design must keep h₀ above the combined surface roughness, any expected debris and a safety margin. h₀ depends on c, ε, viscosity, speed, load, L/D and temperature.
   - Viscosity must be taken at the operating temperature.
   - A lightly loaded or very fast journal hardly moves off centre, so the film is close to a uniform ring of thickness c.
*/
