/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Speed, viscosity, load and clearance act together.
   - Dimensional analysis removes the size and the units.
   - Two bearings with the same value of that number and the same length-to-diameter ratio behave alike, even if one is a 25 mm pump shaft and the other a 250 mm turbine shaft.
   - S is the Sommerfeld number, dimensionless. μ is the dynamic viscosity of the oil at its working temperature, in Pa·s. n is shaft speed in revolutions per second (rev/s). p is the bearing pressure in Pa, p = W/(L D), where W is the load in N
   - Substituting p = W/(L D) removes the pressure and gives the form used when you know the load directly:
   - the symbols are as above; μn is a pressure (Pa), so μn × LD/W is (pressure × area)/force, a pure number.
   - Some books call the group μn/p the bearing characteristic number or bearing modulus.
   - The ratio r/c sits inside a square, so it dominates.
   - A few books print 1/S under the same name.
   - Eccentricity e is the offset of the journal centre from the bush centre; the eccentricity ratio is ε = e/c, and the minimum film thickness is h₀ = c(1 − ε).
*/
