/* book exam traps: Putting n in rpm. The group needs revolutions per second; 1500 rpm is 25 rev/s, and using 1500 inflates S sixty times. | Using πDL as the area. Bearing pressure acts on the projected area L × D, the rectangle you see when you look at the bearing from the side; πDL is the curved surface of the journal and makes p π times too small. | Feeding in the diametral clearance instead of the radial clearance. That halves r/c and cuts S to one quarter. | Forgetting to square r/c. The ratio is around 500, so dropping the square changes S by a factor of 250 000. | Taking viscosity at 40 °C from a grade table. The bearing runs hotter and thinner; S must use the viscosity at the operating temperature. */
